		"fractals": {
			"name": "Fractals: Infinite Within Finite",
			"truth": "Infinite complexity from finite rules. Dimensions between integers. D = log(N)/log(S). Self-similarity across scales.",
			"qfep_term": "E(S) + F",
			"qfep_connection": "Fractals embody the QFEP tension directly: F (deterministic recursive rule) + \u00ce\u00bbE(S) (random variation in stochastic fractals) creates forms at the edge of chaos. The Mandelbrot boundary is where order meets chaos\u00e2\u20ac\u201dinfinitely complex yet bounded. Fractals are queer geometry: dimensionally non-normative, nowhere smooth, computationally irreducible.",
			"formula": "D = log(N)/log(S), z_{n+1} = z_n\u00c2\u00b2 + c",
			"demonstration": "Walk inside the Menger sponge. Watch the stochastic tree grow with random variation. Zoom into the Mandelbrot boundary and see infinite detail.",
			"description": "Self-similarity across scales, infinite complexity from finite rules. Fractals refuse integer dimensions\u00e2\u20ac\u201dthey exist between 1D and 2D, between line and plane. The Mandelbrot boundary is infinitely complex yet bounded; the Sierpinski triangle has zero area yet infinite structure. This is queer geometry: dimensionally non-normative, nowhere smooth, computationally irreducible.",
			"layer": "emergence",
